{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>But on the tenth of the month, etc. — it is Yom HaKippurim.</b> Every אַךְ and רַק is a diminution, as it is stated below: ״וְעִנִּיתֶם אֶת נַפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם בְּתִשְׁעָה לַחֹדֶשׁ בָּעֶרֶב״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Berakhot 8a): But do they fast on the ninth? Behold, they fast on the tenth! Rather, this tells you that whoever eats and drinks on the ninth, Scripture accounts it for him as if he fasted on both the ninth and the tenth. And lest you err and say: since the ninth day too is reckoned as though he fasted on it, perhaps it also atones like the tenth day — Scripture comes and says: ״אַךְ בֶּעָשׂוֹר לַחֹדֶשׁ״ etc., to tell you that the tenth alone atones and not the ninth.\n\nAnd this is more lucid than what Rashi explained, for he explained: it atones for the penitents and does not atone for the non-penitents (see Shevuot 13a) — and would that I knew and could find [the basis] to explain how the expression ״אַךְ״ connotes diminishing the non-penitents, for behold the expression ״אַךְ״ stands upon the tenth day, and its meaning is to diminish the preceding days. And to settle Rashi's explanation I say as follows: since it says ״אַךְ בֶּעָשׂוֹר״, coming to diminish the preceding days — hear from this that even the days before Yom Kippur are called days of repentance. For if not so, from where would you derive that the preceding days atone, such that Scripture needed to diminish them? Rather, it is certainly so that even the ten preceding days are the Ten Days of Repentance, and your initial assumption would be that the Days of Repentance atone immediately for all transgressions — therefore it teaches us that there are transgressions for which repentance suspends [judgment] and Yom Kippur atones, as it is stated (Leviticus 16:30): ״כִּי בַיּוֹם הַזֶּה יְכַפֵּר עֲלֵיכֶם לְטַהֵר אֶתְכֶם מִכֹּל חַטֹּאתֵיכֶם״. Even though they said in the Midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 30:7): on the eve of Rosh HaShanah the Holy One, blessed be He, releases a third, and during the Ten Days of Repentance also a third, but on Yom Kippur He releases everything — therefore it says ״מִכֹּל חַטֹּאתֵיכֶם״ — and from this inference you learn that Yom Kippur does not atone except for the penitents. For if not so, from where would you derive that they atone, such that Scripture needed to diminish them? And even though it is not stated explicitly in the Torah that the preceding days are days of repentance, in any case the shofar of Rosh HaShanah is like a warning that they should repent during the intervening days, as it is written (Amos 3:6): ״אִם יִתָּקַע שׁוֹפָר בְּעִיר וְעָם לֹא יֶחֱרָדוּ״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:16.347511980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}